{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועבדה רבה", :translit "va'avodah rabbah", :gloss "and great substance"} {:he "ב' במסורה", :translit "b' b'masorah", :gloss "two in the Masorah"} {:he "ואידך באיוב", :translit "v'idech b'iyov", :gloss "and the other in Job"} {:he "מה התם גדול מכל בני אדם", :translit "mah sham gadol mikol b'nei adam", :gloss "what there greater than all children of men"} {:he "אף הכא היה גדול מכל", :translit "af haka hayah gadol mikol", :gloss "so too here he was greater than all"} {:he "ואף מאבימלך שהיה מלך", :translit "v'af me'avimelech shehayah melech", :gloss "and even than Abimelech who was a king"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ועבדה רבה" "ועבדה רבה"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase ועבדה רבה occurs exactly twice in Scripture: here describing Isaac's wealth (Genesis 26:14), and in Job 1:3 describing Job's wealth. Job 1:3 explicitly states Job was 'greater than all the children of men.' By the principle of gezerah shavah — matching rare phrases to derive parallel law or meaning — Isaac's greatness is compared to Job's (see mechanism of gezerah shavah codified in Talmud Bavli Shabbat 64a and applied throughout midrashic literature such as Bereshit Rabbah). Abimelech is identified as the Philistine king of Gerar in Genesis 26:1.", :sources ["Genesis 26:14" "Job 1:3" "Genesis 26:1" "Shabbat 64a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ועבדה רבה" "ועבדה רבה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ועבדה רבה", :refs ["Genesis 26:14" "Job 1:3"], :n 2, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ועבדה רבה", :refs ["Genesis 26:14" "Job 1:3"], :n 2, :lens :exact}]}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:21.101664348Z", :model "glm-5.2"}